// 6. HEALTHCARE — COMPASSION WITHOUT COLLAPSE (Dhanvantari)
// ============================================================================
const HEALTHCARE_COMPASSION: ShowroomProgram = {
  slug: "healthcare-compassion-fatigue",
  title: "Compassion Without Collapse",
  subtitle: "Seven days for doctors, nurses, therapists, first responders — staying open in a profession that erodes openness.",
  guru: "Dhanvantari",
  guruRole: "the physician of the gods — the archetype of the healer who does not lose themselves in the healing",
  theme: "🩺",
  color: "from-sky-500/30 via-blue-500/20 to-indigo-500/10",
  category: "nervous",
  audience: "healthcare",
  duration: "7 days",
  promise: "By day 7 you'll have a post-shift decompression ritual, an anti-numbing check, and one honest boundary between compassion and self-erasure.",
  days: [
    { day: 1, title: "Compassion Fatigue Is Not Weakness",
      oneLine: "It is the predictable physiology of a nervous system that has absorbed too much suffering without discharge.",
      reading: `The body of a healthcare worker holds every emergency it witnesses. Without a way to discharge it, the load accumulates. This is not "burnout" (exhaustion from workload). It is *compassion fatigue* — a specific, named clinical condition with markers: numbness to patients, irritability, intrusive images, cynicism.\n\nHealers have been taught to be ashamed of this. Dhanvantari's tradition never was. Ayurveda explicitly names the physician's own regimen as prerequisite to treating anyone.`,
      practice: { name: "The Honest Screening", durationMin: 8,
        instructions: `1. Score yourself 0–10 on: numbness to patient stories / intrusive thoughts about work / irritability at home / avoidance of certain patient types / difficulty sleeping.\n2. Any score above 6 is a real signal, not a character flaw.\n3. Write: "This is compassion fatigue, not who I am."\n4. Keep the note.` },
      reflection: "When did you stop feeling the story of the patient in front of you?" },
    { day: 2, title: "The Threshold Ritual",
      oneLine: "There must be a door between hospital and home. If there isn't, you carry the hospital home.",
      reading: `Healthcare workers who walk from shift to family without a transition arrive as ghosts — physically present, still emotionally in the ICU. The family feels it. Over years, it becomes a distance that never closes.\n\nThe fix is a small, non-negotiable threshold act. Something physical, done in the parking lot or the shower, that means "shift over."`,
      practice: { name: "The Threshold Act", durationMin: 5,
        instructions: `1. Choose one act: change clothes fully / shower before speaking / walk once around the block / one specific song, one time / wash hands slowly with a phrase.\n2. Do it every shift end for 7 days, without exception.\n3. Enter your home only after.` },
      reflection: "How often have you arrived home already elsewhere?" },
    { day: 3, title: "The Patient Is Not You",
      oneLine: "You care deeply. You are also not the patient. The confusion is what breaks healers.",
      reading: `The most compassionate healers develop a specific skill: they let the patient's suffering land in them fully, and they do not fuse with it. They can weep with a family and, an hour later, be fully present with their own child. This is not coldness. It is *differentiation* — the ability to be with without becoming.\n\nWithout it, every patient's death is a small piece of your own, and eventually the accumulated grief is intolerable.`,
      practice: { name: "The Differentiation Line", durationMin: 5,
        instructions: `1. At the end of a patient encounter today, silently name: "Their suffering is theirs. My work with them is mine. I honored it. I return to me."\n2. Physically touch your own chest as you say it.\n3. Do this for every difficult encounter this week.` },
      reflection: "Which patient have you been carrying as if they were you?" },
    { day: 4, title: "The Numbness Check",
      oneLine: "Numbness feels like coping. It isn't. It is anesthesia — and the anesthesia doesn't stay at work.",
      reading: `To keep functioning, healers unconsciously turn down feeling. It works — for the shift. But feeling doesn't turn off surgically. When you numb the ER, you numb your marriage, your parenting, your morning coffee. Whole years pass beige.\n\nThe way back is not to feel everything at work (impossible). It is to have specific places where full feeling is safe again.`,
      practice: { name: "The Feeling Room", durationMin: 15,
        instructions: `1. Choose one weekly place/time where you are allowed to feel fully: therapy / a friend / a walk / journaling / a specific piece of music.\n2. Block it in your calendar.\n3. This week, actually go there. Do not skip it because the shift was hard.\n4. What comes up, comes up.` },
      reflection: "Where in your life have you also gone quiet, not just at work?" },
    { day: 5, title: "The System vs The Vocation",
      oneLine: "You did not become a healer to be broken by charting. Separate the calling from the system.",
      reading: `Much healthcare distress is not the medicine — it is the system: understaffing, EHR clicks, insurance games, admin bloat. Healers often blame themselves ("I'm not cut out for this") for what is a structural failure ("this system is understaffed by 40%").\n\nNaming this clearly is not complaining. It is protecting the calling from the system that would swallow it.`,
      practice: { name: "The Two Columns", durationMin: 15,
        instructions: `1. Column A: "What drains me that is the vocation itself" (real medicine, real death).\n2. Column B: "What drains me that is the system" (charting, staffing, admin).\n3. Look at which column is bigger.\n4. For column B: pick one thing you can push back on institutionally (not fix alone).` },
      reflection: "How much of your exhaustion is medicine, and how much is bureaucracy?" },
    { day: 6, title: "The Wounded Healer, Held",
      oneLine: "You need care too. You have been quietly refusing it because you're the one who gives it.",
      reading: `The oldest archetype in medicine is the wounded healer — from Chiron to Dhanvantari. But the archetype requires the wound to be *tended*, not martyred. A healer who does not receive care becomes brittle, then bitter, then absent.\n\nReceiving care is a skill. Most healers have atrophied it. This week you practice.`,
      practice: { name: "One Received Kindness", durationMin: 10,
        instructions: `1. Ask for one small care this week: a massage / a friend to cook for you / a colleague to cover 30 minutes / a partner to draw a bath.\n2. Receive it without deflecting ("oh you didn't have to") or reciprocating immediately.\n3. Sit with the discomfort of receiving.` },
      reflection: "When did you last let someone care for you without earning it back?" },
    { day: 7, title: "Why You Started",
      oneLine: "Under the exhaustion is a reason. Reconnect with it before the exhaustion swallows it.",
      reading: `Every healer has an origin story — a person they lost, a moment of being helped, a calling. Twenty years of shifts can bury it. The recovery of the story is the recovery of the vocation.\n\nDhanvantari rose from the churning of the ocean holding the pot of *amrita* — the nectar of life. The image is not sentimental. It is a reminder: this work, at its core, is offering something precious to the suffering. Under all the paperwork, that is still what you do.`,
      practice: { name: "The Origin Letter", durationMin: 20,
        instructions: `1. Write a letter to the version of you who decided to enter medicine.\n2. Tell them what you've seen, what has been hard, what you're proud of.\n3. Read it aloud.\n4. Keep it near your workspace.` },
      reflection: "Would the person who chose this work be proud of who you've become inside it?" },
  ],
};

