// 10. GOVERNMENT — INTEGRITY INSIDE THE MACHINE (Vidura)
// ============================================================================
const BUREAUCRAT_INTEGRITY: ShowroomProgram = {
  slug: "government-bureaucrat-integrity",
  title: "Integrity Inside The Machine",
  subtitle: "Seven days for bureaucrats and civil servants — keeping a spine inside a system built to bend it.",
  guru: "Vidura",
  guruRole: "the Mahabharata minister who told kings unwelcome truths and kept his post through it — the archetype of ethical service inside a compromised court",
  theme: "⚖️",
  color: "from-stone-500/30 via-neutral-500/20 to-slate-600/10",
  category: "purpose",
  audience: "government",
  duration: "7 days",
  promise: "By day 7 you'll have a private ethics ledger, a script for the political ask you'll get this month, and a clear-eyed relationship with the length of your career.",
  days: [
    { day: 1, title: "The Long Game Is Yours",
      oneLine: "Politicians rotate. You are the institution. Act on the timescale of the office, not the current occupant.",
      reading: `The single most stabilizing thought for a career civil servant is: the person above me will be gone in 4 years. The next one, and the next one. The institution outlives them. My loyalty is to the institution and the citizens it serves — not to the current minister's re-election.\n\nVidura served across multiple rulers. He was loyal to *Hastinapur*, not to Dhritarashtra personally. That distinction is what kept his integrity when Dhritarashtra's did not.`,
      practice: { name: "The Timescale Card", durationMin: 5,
        instructions: `1. Write: "I serve the office for 30 years. Politicians pass through in 4. My loyalty is to the office and the citizens."\n2. Keep it in your top drawer.\n3. Read it before every meeting where a political ask is coming.` },
      reflection: "Whose approval have you been chasing that will not be here in 5 years?" },
    { day: 2, title: "The Ask You Will Get",
      oneLine: "There is a specific ask coming this month. Rehearse the answer now, in daylight, alone.",
      reading: `Ethical compromises rarely arrive as dramatic Faustian bargains. They arrive as small favors — a file speeded up for a friend of the minister, a tender clause reworded, a data point softened for the press release. Each is small. In sequence, they redefine you.\n\nThe way through is to rehearse the "no" before the ask comes, so it is already in your body when the moment arrives.`,
      practice: { name: "The Rehearsed No", durationMin: 15,
        instructions: `1. Anticipate the likely ask coming this month.\n2. Write your answer: "Sir, I understand the request. That would require me to __ which is outside process. I can offer __ instead."\n3. Say it out loud 5 times in the mirror.\n4. If the ask arrives, deliver the line calmly.` },
      reflection: "Which small compromise have you already made that you told yourself was 'just this once'?" },
    { day: 3, title: "The Ledger",
      oneLine: "Keep a private, dated record of every irregular ask and how you responded. Not for court — for your own sanity.",
      reading: `Officers who kept quiet ledgers for years survived transitions and inquiries that destroyed peers. Not because they used the ledger as ammunition — most never did — but because the *act of writing it* kept them honest with themselves, week after week.\n\nOnce you write down "on this date, X asked for Y, I did Z," you cannot fool yourself about the pattern of your career.`,
      practice: { name: "Start The Ledger", durationMin: 10,
        instructions: `1. Begin a private, secure document today.\n2. First entry: any irregular ask you can remember from the last 6 months.\n3. Format: date / who / what was asked / what I did / what I now think of that.\n4. Add to it weekly. Keep it private. Never on office systems.` },
      reflection: "What have you been unwilling to write down because writing it would make it real?" },
    { day: 4, title: "The Weight of the File",
      oneLine: "Behind every file is a citizen. Behind every delay is a life on hold. Feel it or you'll rot.",
      reading: `Bureaucratic numbness is real and it is fatal to integrity. When files are files, you can delay them, lose them, and stamp them without weight. When you remember there is a widow waiting for a pension, a farmer waiting for a title deed, a family waiting for a permit — the work becomes sacred again.\n\nThis re-humanization is a spiritual discipline. Vidura had it. Most who don't, don't last as ethical officers.`,
      practice: { name: "The Named Citizen", durationMin: 10,
        instructions: `1. Once a day this week, before signing any file, read the citizen's name aloud.\n2. Pause for two seconds.\n3. Then decide.\n4. Notice how the pace of certain files changes.` },
      reflection: "Which file on your desk right now, if it were your parent's, would you have cleared last week?" },
    { day: 5, title: "The Colleague Who Bent",
      oneLine: "Someone you trained with has taken shortcuts and is being rewarded. Do not let their trajectory rewrite yours.",
      reading: `Watching a peer prosper through compromise while you struggle for staying clean is the single hardest test of a public career. The temptation is not usually greed. It is *fairness rage*: "if they can, why can't I."\n\nThe answer is: they are living a different life. It may look shiny from outside. From inside it is fear of the day it unravels, sleep problems, and a family that quietly knows.`,
      practice: { name: "The Trajectory Note", durationMin: 15,
        instructions: `1. Write: "The peers I envy have paid __." Force yourself to list the real prices.\n2. Then write: "The version of me I'm building is __." List what your integrity has bought (sleep, kids' respect, peace of mind).\n3. Read when the envy strikes.` },
      reflection: "Whose trajectory have you been silently competing with without seeing their whole life?" },
    { day: 6, title: "The Family That Pays With You",
      oneLine: "Your integrity is subsidized by your spouse and children. Honor that debt.",
      reading: `An honest officer's family lives with the postings to difficult places, the promotions that didn't come, the offers refused. The officer's ethics are real only because the family absorbs the cost.\n\nMost officers never name this out loud. When they do, the marriage steadies. Silence about the trade is what corrodes it.`,
      practice: { name: "The Named Trade", durationMin: 15,
        instructions: `1. Sit with your partner this week.\n2. Say: "I know my choices at work have cost us __. I see it. I honor it. Here is how I'm going to make it up in what I can control at home."\n3. Then follow through on one small specific thing.` },
      reflection: "What has your family paid for your integrity that you've never acknowledged?" },
    { day: 7, title: "The Retirement Mirror",
      oneLine: "Play the whole reel forward. Sit at retirement. Look back. Whose respect do you want?",
      reading: `Vidura died respected across factions because he had not sold anything. Every officer at retirement knows which of their peers can walk into any bazaar with a straight back and which cannot. That reputation is either built quietly over 30 years or purchased in the last 5 (and everyone can tell the difference).\n\nDecide today whose respect you want at 65. Then live back from it.`,
      practice: { name: "The Retirement Letter", durationMin: 20,
        instructions: `1. Write a paragraph from your retirement day.\n2. Who is at the ceremony? What do they say? What are you proud of? What are you at peace with?\n3. Then: what is one thing this week you can do that keeps that letter true?\n4. Do it.` },
      reflection: "Which decision this month is between the retirement you want and the promotion you want?" },
  ],
};

